{
  // Static-asset Worker for the built Jekyll site. Deployed by
  // .github/workflows/site-worker.yml on every push to main that touches
  // `site/**` or this file — the same push-to-deploy shape as the API
  // Worker's `worker.yml`, using the same two repo secrets
  // (CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN, CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID). No new secrets needed.
  //
  // This is what algosize.com's non-/api/* traffic is served from — see
  // DEPLOY.md §9 "Move algosize.com off GitHub Pages" for the full cutover
  // (Workers Route + DNS + retiring the GitHub Pages deploy).
  //
  // It used to ALSO be deployed by Cloudflare's own "Workers Builds" git
  // integration. RESOLVED 2026-08-20: there were TWO such triggers, both
  // bound to the WRONG service (`algosize`, the API Worker) and both built
  // from the repo root — i.e. from THIS config — so each one deployed the
  // static site onto the API's service. Both are now deleted:
  //
  //   7d4c3cb7-…  "Deploy non-production branches"  every branch except main
  //               deploy_command: npx wrangler versions upload
  //   98c4057e-…  (unnamed; name field was the raw script id)  main only
  //               deploy_command: npx wrangler deploy
  //
  // An earlier version of this comment reasoned that the first was harmless
  // because `versions upload` never routes traffic, making it only a latent
  // risk. That reasoning does not survive what happened on 2026-08-20: over
  // roughly two hours, every push to this repo — feature branches and main
  // alike — left `algosize` serving this site's 404 page instead of the API,
  // with all seven of its secrets wiped, and each manual recovery held only
  // until the next push. Notably the `main` trigger's builds were all marked
  // FAILED in Cloudflare's own build history and still did this, so "the
  // build failed" is not evidence that nothing was deployed.
  //
  // The exact mechanism was never pinned down and does not need to be: the
  // fix is that no Workers Builds trigger may exist on the `algosize`
  // service at all. Verify with:
  //
  //   curl -s ".../accounts/$ACCOUNT/builds/workers/$ALGOSIZE_SCRIPT_ID/triggers" \
  //     -H "Authorization: Bearer $CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN"     # expect result: []
  //
  // Note that endpoint takes the internal script id, NOT the name "algosize"
  // — querying by name returns an empty list whether or not triggers exist,
  // which is why several checks during the incident wrongly read as clean.
  //
  // The GitHub Actions workflow this file's header describes is now the ONLY
  // path that deploys this config. It runs `wrangler deploy` (not `versions
  // upload`) against exactly this config, from a job that only ever touches
  // the service this file names — verified during the incident: its logs
  // show `Uploaded algosize-site` and assert only `staging.algosize.com/*`
  // and `algosize.com/*`, never `algosize.com/api/*`.
  //
  // The service name MUST NOT be `algosize`. That is the API Worker's
  // service (see worker/wrangler.toml), deployed by `worker.yml`. With both
  // configs named `algosize`, the two pipelines would deploy different
  // Workers to the same service and the last one to run would win — so an
  // ordinary site change could silently replace the live API, and an API
  // deploy could replace the site. This is not a hypothetical — it is
  // exactly what the two deleted Workers Builds triggers did on 2026-08-20,
  // by building this config and deploying it onto the API's service.
  //
  // Related: wrangler resolves its config by walking UP the directory tree,
  // so this file also shadows worker/wrangler.toml for any wrangler command
  // run from inside `worker/`. Every wrangler invocation in the repo
  // therefore passes `--config wrangler.toml` explicitly. See DEPLOY.md §2.0.
  //
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The `build` block
  //
  // wrangler's own custom-build hook. It runs BEFORE wrangler reads
  // `assets.directory`, so the directory exists by the time it is needed —
  // `_site_build` is a gitignored artifact, not a committed one, and nothing
  // else in CI produces it.
  //
  // It runs even though this Worker has no `main`. An assets-only config
  // resolves its entry point to wrangler's bundled templates/no-op-worker.js
  // (see resolveEntryWithAssets in the wrangler source), and runCustomBuild
  // is called immediately after that resolution — the entry-point assertion
  // then passes trivially because the no-op template always exists.
  //
  // The command mirrors .github/workflows/jekyll.yml exactly — same config
  // pair, same JEKYLL_ENV — so what deploys here is identical to what the
  // GitHub Pages build produces (Pages is still live; see `routes` below
  // and DEPLOY.md §9 for the plan to retire it). `cwd` is relative to the
  // repo root, which is where `wrangler deploy` is invoked from.
  //
  // The Ruby version this runs under is whatever site-worker.yml pins via
  // ruby/setup-ruby — deliberately NOT auto-detected the way Cloudflare's
  // build image did. Ruby 3.4 quietly moved `csv` and `base64` out of
  // default gems into bundled ones, and this exact build broke on it with
  // "cannot load such file -- csv" before site/Gemfile declared them
  // explicitly. Pinning the Ruby version in CI is what stops that class of
  // bug from recurring on some future Ruby release we haven't tested against.
  "$schema": "worker/node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "algosize-site",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-05-11",
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  },
  "build": {
    "cwd": "site",
    "command": "bundle install && JEKYLL_ENV=production bundle exec jekyll build --config _config.yml,_config.production.yml --destination ../_site_build"
  },
  "assets": {
    "directory": "_site_build",
    // Jekyll's default permalinks are directory-style — a post at
    // /blog/foo/ builds to blog/foo/index.html on disk. "auto-trailing-slash"
    // is what makes a request for /blog/foo (no slash) or /blog/foo/ both
    // resolve to that file, matching how GitHub Pages served the same build.
    "html_handling": "auto-trailing-slash",
    // site/404.html renders the real page for anything unmatched, instead of
    // an empty 404 response with no body.
    "not_found_handling": "404-page"
  },
  // A Cloudflare Workers Route, applied on every `wrangler deploy` — the
  // same mechanism worker/wrangler.toml already uses for
  // `algosize.com/api/*` (see DEPLOY.md §4.2). No dashboard click: this
  // config, reviewed in a diff, IS the route.
  //
  // Deliberately staging ONLY. `staging.algosize.com/api/*` already routes
  // to the staging API Worker (worker/wrangler.toml [env.staging]) — more
  // specific patterns win, so that keeps working unchanged; everything else
  // on that hostname now comes from this Worker instead of falling through
  // to GitHub Pages. This is DEPLOY.md §9.2, the full-flow rehearsal: it
  // costs nothing (zero production traffic) and is safe to ship any time.
  //
  // The THIRD entry — `algosize.com/*` — is §9.3, the production cutover.
  // From here, algosize.com's non-/api/* traffic is served by this Worker,
  // not GitHub Pages: routed requests are handled entirely at Cloudflare's
  // edge and never reach the GitHub Pages origin, so this took effect the
  // moment this deploy landed, with no DNS change. `algosize.com/api/*`
  // (worker/wrangler.toml) keeps winning over this catch-all for its own
  // requests — same specificity rule already proven by that route, which
  // has been live on this exact zone all along.
  //
  // Rollback: revert this entry (or the whole commit) and redeploy —
  // `worker/node_modules/.bin/wrangler deploy --config wrangler.jsonc` from
  // the repo root if you don't want to wait for CI. GitHub Pages has not
  // been touched by any of this, so reverting the route is a complete,
  // instant undo. See DEPLOY.md §9.4 for retiring Pages once this has been
  // live and quiet for a while — not done yet, do it separately.
  "routes": [
    { "pattern": "staging.algosize.com/*", "zone_name": "algosize.com" },
    { "pattern": "algosize.com/*", "zone_name": "algosize.com" }
  ],
  // Explicit rather than relying on the default. Wrangler's default is
  // `workers_dev = routes.length === 0` — true when routes is empty, false
  // the moment a route is added. Without this, adding the staging route
  // above would silently kill the workers.dev preview URL that DEPLOY.md
  // §9.1 uses to confirm the Worker deploys and serves correctly on its own,
  // independent of any route.
  "workers_dev": true,
  "compatibility_flags": [
    "nodejs_compat"
  ]
}
